---
// src/pages/agents.astro — SEO landing page: Root My Android for AI agents.
// Keyword target: the "AI agent API" / "MCP server" cluster — agent-side
// discovery + LLM/GEO visibility. Companion to /mcp (owned by the
// rootmyandroid-api Worker): /mcp is the endpoint + machine-generated tool
// reference; THIS page is the explainer. CONTENT page: zero JavaScript
// (AGENTS.md rule 4). All endpoint facts verified live 2026-11-07 against
// GET/POST https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp (initialize + tools/list) and the
// /mcp HTML catalog; plan facts from src/pages/pricing.astro. This page
// deliberately describes capabilities, not a frozen tool table, so the two
// never drift apart.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

// Connect snippets, rendered verbatim in the <pre> blocks below (kept in
// the frontmatter so literal JSON braces never collide with the template).
const mcpConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "rootmyandroid": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp"
    }
  }
}`
const rpcInit = `curl -sS https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -H 'accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \\
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{
        "protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},
        "clientInfo":{"name":"my-agent","version":"1.0.0"}}}'`

// FAQ entries render visibly in the <details> list below AND feed the FAQPage
// JSON-LD — the two must stay text-identical (structured data mirrors copy,
// same pattern as src/pages/index.astro).
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does Root My Android have an MCP server?',
    a: 'Yes. The server speaks the Model Context Protocol — JSON-RPC 2.0 over the Streamable HTTP transport — at https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp. It is stateless, requires no API key, and its tools cover accounts, subscription plans, card and crypto checkout, vouchers, payment status, and human support.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can an AI agent create an account without a human?',
    a: 'Yes. The create_account tool (or POST /v1/accounts on the REST API) creates a Mullvad-style 16-digit account with no email, no password, and no personal data. The account number itself is the credential, so an agent can onboard a user end-to-end and hand them just the number.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can an agent take payments on behalf of a user?',
    a: 'Yes, on two rails. create_card_checkout returns a hosted invoice URL to hand to the user at the full fiat price; create_crypto_checkout returns a deposit address, amount, and currency with a 15% discount on listed prices. get_payment_status then reconciles the payment authoritatively, even if the webhook never arrives.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an API key or registration to call the API?',
    a: 'No. Both the MCP server and the REST API at rootmyandroid.com/v1 are public: no API key, no signup, no OAuth. Where an operation acts on an account, the account number is the credential.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where is the full MCP tool reference?',
    a: 'At https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp. A GET request returns a human-readable page whose tools table is generated from the same catalog as the MCP tools/list method, so it always matches what the server actually exposes.',
  },
]

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
}
---

<Base
  title="For AI Agents: MCP Server & Agent API"
  description="Root My Android for AI agents: a public MCP server (JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP) and REST API for no-PII accounts, Guardian Angel subscriptions, card & crypto checkout, vouchers, and support."
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="container" style="padding: 3rem 1.25rem">
    <h1 style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem">Root My Android for AI agents</h1>
    <p class="muted" style="margin: 0 0 1.5rem; font-size: 1.1rem; max-width: 44rem">
      Root My Android is built to be driven by software, not just visited
      by people. A public REST API and a
      <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</a>
      server expose every customer-facing service — Mullvad-style accounts,
      Guardian Angel subscriptions, card &amp; crypto checkout, vouchers,
      payment status, and human support — to any agent. No API key, no
      registration, no personal data required anywhere.
    </p>

    <nav class="panel toc" aria-label="On this page" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#why">Why Root My Android is agent-friendly</a></li>
        <li><a href="#connect">Connect your agent (MCP server)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#capabilities">What agents can do</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rest-api">The REST API behind it</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="why">Why Root My Android is agent-friendly</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Accounts without identity.</strong> Accounts are
          Mullvad-style 16-digit numbers — there is no email, password,
          phone, or KYC step anywhere in the flow. Those identity gates are
          exactly where agents usually get stuck; here they do not exist.
          An agent can create an account for its user and act on it
          indefinitely with just the number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Public by design.</strong> No API keys, no OAuth apps, no
          approval waitlists for the API itself — the exposed services are
          public, and an account number is the only credential where one is
          needed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>MCP-native.</strong> One well-known URL speaks the Model
          Context Protocol; any MCP-capable client (Claude, Cursor, or a
          custom agent) connects by adding the server to its config.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Money rails agents can actually use.</strong> The card
          rail returns a hosted invoice URL to hand to the user, and the
          crypto rail settles without a bank account — 15% off listed
          prices, enforced server-side.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Self-healing payments.</strong> Payment status is
          reconciled authoritatively on the server, so a missed webhook
          never strands a user's credit.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="connect">Connect your agent (MCP server)</h2>
      <p>
        The endpoint is <code>POST https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp</code>
        (a plain GET returns the human-readable catalog). Add the server to
        any MCP-capable client:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{mcpConfig}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Or drive it by hand — every message is one POST with a JSON-RPC 2.0
        body, and responses are single <code>application/json</code>
        documents: no SSE streams, no session headers, no batching.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{rpcInit}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Protocol facts: the server is <code>rootmyandroid-mcp</code>
        v0.1.0; protocol versions 2025-06-18, 2025-03-26, and 2024-11-05
        are negotiated on <code>initialize</code>; the transport is
        stateless — no <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> is issued; capabilities
        are tools only (no resources, no prompts). The
        <a href="https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp" rel="noopener">/mcp page</a>
        itself is the always-current reference — its tools table is
        generated from the same catalog as <code>tools/list</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="capabilities">What agents can do</h2>
      <p>
        14 tools at the time of writing (November 2026). This is a
        capability map — the exact, machine-generated reference lives at
        <a href="https://rootmyandroid.com/mcp" rel="noopener">/mcp</a>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Accounts &amp; entitlements:</strong>
          <code>create_account</code>, <code>get_account</code>,
          <code>get_entitlements</code>, and <code>verify_feature</code> —
          the gate every tier feature checks: is feature X unlocked for
          this account right now?
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plans &amp; checkout:</strong>
          <code>list_subscription_plans</code> (flat prepaid Guardian Angel
          tiers with full-fiat and 15%-discounted crypto prices),
          <code>create_card_checkout</code> (hosted invoice URL),
          <code>create_crypto_checkout</code> (whitelabel deposit address,
          amount, and currency), and <code>get_payment_status</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vouchers:</strong> <code>redeem_voucher</code> — a
          single-use code that credits its days of Guardian Angel PRO to an
          account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Human support:</strong>
          <code>open_support_conversation</code>
          (<code>realtime</code> = anonymous live chat;
          <code>message</code> = asynchronous, requires an email),
          <code>send_support_message</code>, and
          <code>poll_support_messages</code>. Conversations opened via MCP
          share one 5/day quota, because all MCP calls originate from the
          same Worker.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Diagnostics &amp; discovery:</strong>
          <code>get_network_identity</code> (your egress IP, user agent,
          ASN, TLS version, and geolocation echoed from Cloudflare edge
          metadata — nothing is stored) and <code>get_api_index</code>
          (every public REST endpoint).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="rest-api">The REST API behind it</h2>
      <p>
        Every MCP tool is a thin proxy of the public REST API at
        <code>rootmyandroid.com/v1</code>, which owns all validation and
        business logic. Device-facing endpoints (encrypted backups,
        Guardian Angel pairing, audit snapshots) remain REST-only for now —
        they need an on-device context an agent cannot fabricate.
      </p>
      <p>
        What agents sell or manage: Guardian Angel monitoring for up to 5
        devices per account — Lite at $5 / 30 days, Pro at $15 / 30 days;
        subscribers can switch between Lite and Pro anytime, and crypto
        payments save 15%. Full details on our
        <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section id="faq" class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 2.5rem">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="panel faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p class="muted">{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </section>

    <p class="faint small" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 44rem">
      Android is a trademark of Google LLC. All other product names
      mentioned (including Claude and Cursor) are the property of their
      respective owners and are referenced here for identification and
      informational purposes only. Last updated: November 2026.
    </p>

    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  </article>

  <style>
    .toc h2 {
      margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    }
    .toc ol {
      margin: 0;
      padding-left: 1.25rem;
      display: grid;
      gap: 0.35rem;
    }
    /* FAQ: native <details> accordions — zero JavaScript. */
    .faq-item summary {
      cursor: pointer;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .faq-item p {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
